      "rev_n_17_c79832_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_17_c79832_0",
        "node_id": "n_17_c79832",
        "thought_text": "Since N is up to 10^5, an O(N^2) approach is too slow; we must ensure the solution runs in linear time relative to N.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7836963594197619265",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506243.944371
      },
      "rev_n_18_9db603_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_18_9db603_0",
        "node_id": "n_18_9db603",
        "thought_text": "The problem guarantees we can replace any number with any integer, so the optimal strategy is simply to maximize the GCD of the remaining N-1 numbers, which can always be achieved by setting the replaced number to that GCD.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7836963594197619265",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506243.94438
      },
      "rev_n_19_ac9e2a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_19_ac9e2a_0",
        "node_id": "n_19_ac9e2a",
        "thought_text": "Compute prefix GCDs and suffix GCDs in two passes, then iterate through each element to find the maximum GCD of the remaining N-1 elements by combining the corresponding prefix and suffix values.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7759036686237790461",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506259.7700922
      },
      "rev_n_20_cab3fd_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_20_cab3fd_0",
        "node_id": "n_20_cab3fd",
        "thought_text": "Optimize space complexity by calculating the total GCD first, then for each element, compute the GCD of the rest by re-scanning or using a single pass with running GCD updates if N is small, though the prefix/suffix method is generally safer for large N.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7759036686237790461",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794506259.7701302
      },
      "rev_n_21_f8f774_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_21_f8f774_0",
        "node_id": "n_21_f8f774",
        "thought_text": "Instead of checking every element removal, generate candidate GCDs from the prime factorizations of all numbers, count their occurrences, and verify if removing one number leaves at least N-1 numbers divisible by the candidate, which might be faster if the number of distinct prime factors is small.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-7759036686237790461",
